---
scope: src/tools/init
intent: >
  aide_init is the bootstrap — the one command that turns a cold
  project into an AIDE-ready project. It auto-detects the host agent
  framework, writes the AIDE methodology into the framework's config
  file, scaffolds a slash command for every pipeline phase, wires the
  MCP server into the project's MCP config, and configures the IDE so
  `.aide` files render as Markdown. Every step is idempotent so the
  tool is safe to re-run, and every step reports its own status so the
  caller can see exactly what changed.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - Auto-detection picks the right framework (Claude Code, Cursor,
      Windsurf, Copilot-in-VSCode) from files already on disk, and the
      caller can override the detection explicitly when they need to.
    - The methodology block is written into the framework's canonical
      config file (e.g. CLAUDE.md for Claude Code) exactly once.
      Subsequent runs detect the block's marker and skip the write.
    - Slash commands are scaffolded one file per pipeline phase into
      the framework's command directory. Existing command files are
      left untouched; only missing ones are created.
    - The MCP config is patched to register the `aide` server. If the
      file exists and parses, the server is added to its `mcpServers`
      map; if the file is missing, a minimal config is created. An
      existing `aide` entry is left alone.
    - IDE configuration runs by default and can be skipped with a
      flag. Zed gets a `.zed/settings.json` patch; VS Code gets the
      bundled `.vsix` installed through the `code` CLI when that CLI
      is available. Missing CLI is a skip, not an error.
    - Every step's status — created, wired, installed, exists,
      skipped — is reported in the final summary. A fully-cold run
      reads as a list of creates; a fully-warm run reads as a list of
      "already exists".
    - Re-running the tool on a project that is already fully
      initialized returns a summary saying so and changes nothing on
      disk.
  undesired:
    - An init that appends the methodology block to the config file on
      every run, so a project initialized three times contains three
      copies of the block. Idempotency is an invariant, not a
      nice-to-have.
    - An init that overwrites an existing slash command because "the
      template is the canonical version". The user may have customized
      it. Customizations survive init.
    - An init that clobbers the `mcpServers` map when it fails to
      parse the existing MCP config, destroying every other server
      the user had configured. Parse failure is a skip, not a rewrite.
    - An init that fails the whole run when any single step fails. IDE
      configuration failing because `code` is not on PATH should not
      prevent the methodology, commands, and MCP wiring from being
      written.
    - An init that reaches for the network — downloading a `.vsix`,
      fetching a template, contacting a marketplace. The tool must
      succeed offline, because the developer running it may have no
      network and because network calls turn a local operation into a
      flaky one.
    - An init whose success output is a single word ("done") or a
      serialized status object. The caller needs to see exactly which
      steps created files and which were skipped, because that is
      the information they will paste into a commit or cite to a
      teammate.
---

## Context

Every new project that adopts AIDE has to cross the same cold-start
gap: methodology documented where the agent will see it, pipeline slash
commands scaffolded, the MCP server registered, and the IDE taught to
render `.aide` as Markdown. Without a single tool that does all of
this, each project rediscovers the list and gets one or two steps
wrong — a methodology written in the wrong file, a slash command with
a typo, an MCP config that registers the server under the wrong name,
an IDE that still renders `.aide` as plain text. This tool exists to
collapse the cold-start gap into one command, so the decision to adopt
AIDE in a new project is trivially reversible and costs nothing to try.

The tool composes private helpers that live as subfolders of this
orchestrator: framework detection, methodology and command template
content, the three idempotent write steps (`writeMethodology`,
`scaffoldCommands`, `wireMcp`), and IDE configuration. Init itself is
thin — it orchestrates those helpers, sequences their results, and
shapes the final summary. The strategic decisions about *how* to
configure each IDE, or *which* files count as a framework's canonical
config, live next to the helpers that make those decisions.

## Strategy

**Each step is independently idempotent and independently reportable.**
Every step — methodology write, command scaffold, MCP wire, Zed patch,
VS Code install — knows how to detect its own "already done" state and
reports `exists` when it finds one. The steps do not share state, so a
step that fails or skips cannot corrupt the steps around it, and the
final summary can honestly report a mix of creates and skips in the
same run.

**Failures in one step do not abort the others.** IDE configuration is
the canonical example: a developer without the `code` CLI should still
get a fully-configured methodology, commands, and MCP wiring. The tool
treats each step as best-effort and surfaces the mix of outcomes in
the summary. The alternative — abort on first failure — would make
init brittle in exactly the environments where it needs to be
reliable.

**Host framework detection is a read-only inspection.** The detector
looks at files already on disk (a `.claude` directory, a `cursor`
config, a `.vscode/settings.json` with Copilot-ish keys) and returns
the first match. Nothing is written during detection, and the caller
can always override the result with an explicit `framework` parameter.
This keeps detection safe to run speculatively and makes the override
path a first-class escape hatch.

**Network is never the happy path.** Every asset init needs is checked
into the repo — the methodology text, the command templates, the
bundled `.vsix`. A developer running init on a laptop with no network
gets the same result as a developer running it online. Anything that
would require a network round-trip is a design bug in this tool, not
a feature to add.

**Report the full transition, not just success or failure.** The
summary names each step, labels its status, and orders them in
execution order. The caller reads the summary once and knows exactly
what changed on disk, which files to re-read, and which steps were
already done. A cold run and a warm run produce the same shape of
output — the only difference is which status words appear.

## Good examples

A developer runs `aide_init` on a fresh project in Claude Code and
sees:

> AIDE initialized (claude framework):
>
>   ✓ Methodology: Created
>   ✓ aide-research: Created
>   ✓ aide-spec: Created
>   ✓ aide-build: Created
>   ✓ aide-qa: Created
>   ✓ aide-fix: Created
>   ✓ MCP config: Wired
>   ✓ Zed config: Created
>   ✓ VS Code extension: Installed
>
> Config: CLAUDE.md
> Commands: .claude/commands
> MCP: .mcp.json
>
> New commands: aide-research, aide-spec, aide-build, aide-qa, aide-fix
>
> Next steps: run aide_discover to see existing specs, or /aide-research to start a new one.

Every step accounted for, every path named, and a pointer to the next
action. The developer reads one screen and knows the project is ready.

A developer re-runs the same command on the same project and sees:

> AIDE already initialized (claude framework detected).
>
> All components present:
>   - Methodology
>   - aide-research
>   - aide-spec
>   - aide-build
>   - aide-qa
>   - aide-fix
>   - MCP config
>   - Zed config
>   - VS Code extension
>
> Run aide_discover to see existing specs.

Nothing on disk changed, and the output says so plainly. The re-run
was safe.

A developer without the `code` CLI runs `aide_init` and the VS Code
step is reported as `Skipped` while every other step still runs to
completion. They install `code`, re-run init, and the VS Code step
flips to `Installed` while every other step reports `Already exists`.
The tool is composable with the developer's environment, not
adversarial to it.

## Bad examples

An init that appends the methodology block to CLAUDE.md on every run,
producing a file with three stacked copies after three runs. The
idempotency invariant has been violated and the config file is now
corrupted.

An init that overwrites `.claude/commands/aide-build.md` with the
bundled template, wiping out the user's customizations. The tool has
no way to know what the customizations were for, and the user finds
out by reading a diff they did not expect.

An init that catches a parse error on the existing `.mcp.json` and
responds by writing a fresh minimal config, destroying the user's
other MCP server registrations. The right move is to skip the wire
step and report the skip in the summary.

An init that aborts the whole run because `code --version` exited
non-zero. The developer wanted a configured methodology, commands,
and MCP config; what they got was an error and an unconfigured
project, because one optional step was missing an optional
dependency.

An init whose summary reads `done`. The developer has no idea which
files changed, whether the methodology was written, whether the
slash commands were new or pre-existing, or which IDE the tool
configured. The single word is worse than no output, because it
implies a report was generated.
